\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Definition}
\title{Finite incidence data and real fields}
\date{2026-10-16}

\p{Specialize the [ring-valued incidence fields](fdip-0010) to
#{R=\mathbb R}. The finite sets #{V,E} and endpoint maps #{s,h:E\to V}
form a \newvocab{finite incidence presentation}; its real specialization
adds the scalar order needed for positive pairings and weights.
A \newvocab{real vertex field} is a function #{f:V\to\mathbb R}.
Write #{\mathbb R^V=\mathcal F_{\mathbb R}(V)} and
#{\mathbb R^E=\mathcal F_{\mathbb R}(E)} for the vertex and edge spaces.}

\p{Their \newvocab{counting inner products} are
##{\langle f,g\rangle_V=\sum_{v\in V}f(v)g(v),\qquad
   \langle a,b\rangle_E=\sum_{e\in E}a(e)b(e).}
These finite sums are bilinear and symmetric. A sum of squares vanishes
exactly when all its entries vanish, so they are positive definite,
including on the zero space when an indexing set is empty. The real field
values remain continuous even though the carrier is finite.}

\p{The real \newvocab{incidence map} is the specialization
##{D=d_{\mathbb R}:\mathbb R^V\longrightarrow\mathbb R^E,\qquad
   (Df)(e)=f(h(e))-f(s(e)).}
Its linearity follows from \ref{fdip-0010}. Loops have zero
incidence, and parallel edges remain distinct. For an oriented loopless
graph this is the sign convention of
\citet{Section 2.1, p. 4}{spielman2009effective}; the endpoint formula
extends it to loops. Connectedness and positive weights are not needed
to define #{D}.}

\p{A positive edge-weight function #{w:E\to\mathbb R_{>0}} is additional
data. It acts on edge fields by #{(Wa)(e)=w(e)a(e)}. Different weights
change the supplied data; no retained subset is part of this definition.
The [four-cycle example](fdip-0003) recovers coordinate arrays from
these maps and specifies a retained subset separately.}

\p{For the counting inner product, Mathlib's
\leanref{EuclideanSpace} is the corresponding finite function space and
\leanref{PiLp.inner_apply} gives the sum of coordinate inner products.
These declarations concern the pairing, rather than the incidence
presentation as a whole.}
